00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:06.156
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.156 --> 00:00:11.140
Previously, we discussed how to compare
two sequences with each other.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.330
This is pairwise alignment.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:18.020
And now, let's discuss how to find
multiple sequence alignment.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.670
So how to compare multiple sequences at
once.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:22.560
Why do we need this?

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.830
Statistically significant similarities
between two sequences becomes

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.530
more significant if it presents in many

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:36.310
other sequences.
So if you compare a lot of similar

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:42.190
species and you find very conservative
domain in this species,

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.130
it is very significant.
While, when you compare these species

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:53.220
pairwise, this conservative sequence may
be not so obvious.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.250
For example, when you align three
A-domains,

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:03.290
which we discussed previously in this
lesson.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.465
You can find many pairwise similarities
between these three

00:01:10.465 --> 00:01:15.150
A-domains, but when you're trying to
compare all of them at once, you will

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:20.210
find that similarity, which present

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:25.310
between all of these three sequences, and
which can say you

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.830
something about what makes this sequences
behave similarly

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.710
in a cell.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.550
So let's generalize pairwise alignment to
multiple alignment.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:44.220
Previously we used two row matrix for
alignment of two sequences.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.790
Now, when they want to represent alignment

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.620
of three sequences, let's use three-row
matrix.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.910
And we will need to design our scoring
function to score alignments with more

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:01.060
conserved columns higher than other ones.
And

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.950
our pairwise alignment was a path in a two
dimensional Manhattan grid.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.110
And alignment between three sequences,
actually

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.680
it's a path in three dimensional grid.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:21.204
For example, let's take a look on
alignment of ATGC, AATC, and ATGC.

00:02:22.718 --> 00:02:29.062
Here you can see the number of symbols up
to given position

00:02:29.062 --> 00:02:33.576
written in a separate vector, and for
first

00:02:33.576 --> 00:02:38.700
sequence, it's, it's 0, 1, 1, 2, 3,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.684
4.
Then for a second sequence, it's 0 1 2 3 3

00:02:43.684 --> 00:02:48.673
4.
And 0 0 1 2 3 4 for the last sequence.

00:02:48.673 --> 00:02:54.100
And we can take columns.
The first column of

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.690
this vector.
The second column of this vectors.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.444
And so on and so forth as a points in
three dimensional space.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.304
And our alignment paths will move from
0,0,0 point, which is

00:03:08.304 --> 00:03:14.336
source in our three dimensional Manhattan
grid, to 1,1,0, to

00:03:14.336 --> 00:03:21.030
1,2,1, and so on and so forth.
And it will finish at the

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.010
opposite corner of this three dimensional
grid as

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.320
it started, and this opposite corner, we
will also call it, is a

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.660
sim similarly to our two dimensional
Manhattan grid.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.189
And two dimensional alignment

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:48.820
during each step we took maximum

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.950
of three values, which were vertically,
diagonally,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:59.440
and horizontally opposite on this
alignment cell.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.530
But in multiple dimension space, you will
have more neighbors for each cell.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.870
For example, for three dimensional
alignment cell, you

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:13.480
will need to take a maximum of seven
values.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.910
And here is dynamic programming equations
for

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:25.470
aligning three sequences.
You will need to find

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:30.830
a maximum among all seven neighbors plus

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:35.290
score of moving from this neighbor to the
current

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:39.680
node.
And how about run

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.900
time of such algorithm?

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.150
So for three sequences of length n, run
time

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.420
is proportional to the number of edges in
this grid.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.380
Similar as before, but the number of edges
now is much larger.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.900
So it's 7 times n cubed, because each of

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:03.330
n cubed node, you'll have seven neighbors
to choose from.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.970
So ky alignment, where you want to find
alignment between k

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:16.060
sequences, will build a k dimensional
Manhattan graph, with n

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.874
To the power of k nodes, because it's a
k-dimensional

00:05:20.874 --> 00:05:25.222
cube, or k-dimensional rectangle.

00:05:25.222 --> 00:05:29.890
And most nodes, except of border ones,
will have 2

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:33.600
to the power of k minus 1 incoming edges.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.260
So overall run time is proportional to the
2 to

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.620
the power of k times n to the power of k.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:47.740
And this is really large, so this
algorithm will work for a small

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:53.550
sequences, small number of sequences, or
many sequences of small length.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.270
But it won't work in a

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:00.570
feasible time for many long sequences.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:05.680
For example, to find a multiple alignment
of 18 sequences of

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:11.680
lengths 18 each, you will need more time
than the age of universe.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.050
And biologists usually use fast heuristic
algorithms.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:21.080
They, they don't guarantee the find
optimal solutions, but they guarantee

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:26.990
that this algorithm will finish at a
meaningful time.

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.550
Let's discuss few heuristics which will
help us

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.330
to find multiple alignment in more
feasible way.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.290
Every multiple alignment in use pairwise
alignment, you can take two rows

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:46.600
by any way you want, and you will get
pairwise alignment of these two

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.639
sequences, which is induced from this mul,
multiple alignment.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.160
But can we find multiple alignment from
pairwise alignments?

00:06:57.918 --> 00:07:03.190
In generally, no, because given a set of
arbitrary pairwise

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:09.290
alignments, we cannot construct multiple
alignment that induces them.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:11.910
For example if we have sequences

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:17.950
AAATTT, TTTGGG, and GGGAAA, we have this

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.850
three pairwise alignments, which have,
four matches

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:30.150
each, but we cannot construct multiple
alignment of this three sequences

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.130
which will induce all these three pairwise
alignment.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.760
But what can we do?

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.830
Let's consider profile representation of
multiple alignment.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.610
We had profiles before in this course.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:51.160
And profile is usually a probability for
each

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:56.710
letter to occur in each column.
So for these sequences, you can see that

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:01.940
first column contains 0 A's, 1 T, 3 C,

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:08.160
and 1 gap.
And probabilities of C

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:15.380
is 0.6.
Probability of T and the gap is 0.2.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:21.090
So how can we align sequence against
sequence?

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.370
You already know this.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:27.040
This is what Pavel told before in this
lecture.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:33.150
But can we align sequence against profile?
Or can we align profile against profile?

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:40.850
yes, actually, we can.
So aligning sequence against sequence

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:46.270
requires you to build this Manhattan graph
and write down one sequence

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:51.320
horizontally, and one sequence vertically,
but you can

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:56.940
replace the sequence with a profile and
you can use values

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:02.430
proportional to the values in the profile
to score each

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:06.830
edge in a graph.
Also, you can use profile as a second

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:11.660
sequence.
And you can align these two profiles in

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:16.240
a very similar way that you aligned two
sequences with each other.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:22.412
But for scoring function, you need to also
consider probabilities, to

00:09:22.412 --> 00:09:27.590
have a certain letter in certain

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:32.970
sequence at certain place.
So how we can use this fact

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:39.130
to find multiple alignment.
We can construct simple greedy algorithm.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:44.620
We will calculate all pairwise alignments
between all sequences.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.800
For example, we have k sequences, as

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.610
a start, we will calculate all pairwise
alignments

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.020
of these k sequences, which is quadratic

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.700
number of, which is proportional to the
quadratic

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.650
number of these sequences, and we will

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:07.710
find two closest sequences among all of
them.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:11.780
And we will join these two sequences into
our file.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:14.830
After this step, we will have k minus 1
sequences.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.770
Well, actually we will have k minus

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:21.140
two sequences in one profile, but we know
that we

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:25.500
can align profile in the same way as we
align sequences.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.990
And we will iterate, so on each iteration
will reduce number

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:36.860
of sequences and profiles to align between
each other by one.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:41.860
For example, let's take these four
sequences, GATTCA,

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:48.288
GTCTGA, GATATT, and GTCAGC.

00:10:48.288 --> 00:10:53.200
We'll find six pairwise alignments to the
sequences and the best

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:58.260
one is alignment between second and fourth
sequences and

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:03.180
it has score two.
So we will join this

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.410
two sequences into one profile and it will
add to our

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:12.693
pool of sequences we will want to align.
And now, we have new set of three

00:11:12.693 --> 00:11:17.829
sequences to align, sequence one, which is
originally

00:11:17.829 --> 00:11:22.530
sequence one, sequence three, and new
profile.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.090
And we can iterate.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:29.060
We can construct pairwise alignments
between each

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:32.982
of these three sequences, and find closest
and

00:11:32.982 --> 00:11:39.060
join them and so on and so forth, and at
the last step, we will get to

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:44.700
a single profile of multiple alignment.
This greedy algorithm is not optimal.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:49.670
It may find not optimal solution, but it
will work fast.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:54.600
And it will work on most of the real world
cases.

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.000
So today we learned a lot about sequence
alignment.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:03.920
We learned how to find pairwise alignment
in quadratic time and linear space.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.940
We learned how to use a find get

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:12.360
analysis, which represent real world
better for biological problems.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:16.300
We learned how to find global and local
alignments.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:21.210
We learned how to find multiple alignment
between many sequences at once.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.560
But can you find all similarities between

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:25.800
human, mouse, and rat genomes, given

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.730
that these genomes contain billions of
nucleotides?

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:34.390
Can you rapidly find alignments between
human genome and millions of short reads?

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:39.660
Which usually obtain by sequences as
genome.

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:42.730
And are alignment algorithms with
quadratic

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:46.500
time practical when we analyze entire
genomes?

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.030
Because as I said mammal

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.610
genomes are really large.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:56.560
And when you try to analyze these genomes,
quadratic time usually not works.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.480
And these questions we will discuss in
future chapters.

